    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d7">

                <pb facs="0019" n="9" sameAs="frus:frus1937v05/pg9"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">724.34119/836</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the American Delegate</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Braden</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-05-07">May 7, 1937</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to your despatch No. 406 of
                    April 20, 1937, following are the Department’s views with respect to the
                    specific recommendations made by you regarding the further course of
                    negotiations.</p>
                <p>The necessity for a vigorous effort to reach a settlement on the territorial
                    issue has been urged upon both the Argentine and Brazilian Ambassadors during
                    conversations with them at the Department. This policy will be continued
                    whenever the opportunity arises.</p>
                <p>The Department would be prepared to send a message to the Paraguayan Government
                    as indicated in paragraph (<hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>) on page 12 of <pb
                        facs="0020" n="10" xml:id="pg10"/> your despatch, contingent upon the
                    situation calling for such action and upon circumstances being opportune.</p>
                <p>Whenever the Paraguayan Minister has come to the Department, occasion has been
                    taken to talk with him about the desirability of reaching a definitive
                    settlement of the territorial issue. This procedure also will be continued.</p>
                <p>The Department is of the opinion that the trade agreements program cannot be used
                    in any effective manner to induce favorable action in the Chaco negotiations
                    upon the part of the Paraguayan Government.</p>
                <p>Very truly yours,</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sumner
                            Welles</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
